This paper focuses on a family of nonlinear parabolic equations given by 
\begin{equation*}
\partial_t u + \Delta u + |u|^{2^* - 2} u = 0 \quad \text{in} \quad \mathbb{R}^N \times (0,\infty), 
\end{equation*}
where $N \geq 11$ and $u$ initializes as a compact support solution. We establish that for compact support solutions, there are no Type II blowups, and demonstrate that this outcome is reached by analyzing a key inequality involving a critical Sobolev constant and a precise estimate for a key function. This result expands upon previous findings of Type II blowup nonexistence discovered by Wei and Xie (2019), and Cazanadel and Muller (2020). Our proof relies on a refined analysis of the blowup rate and a novel adaptation of a method developed by Wei and Xie. This approach may be applied to other problems in the field of parabolic equations. We also explore the connection between our work and a broader category of Type II problems, and discuss potential avenues for future research. The investigation into our parabolic equation also yields insights into its corresponding static problem. This paper concludes with a number of open questions and potential areas of further investigation. The accompanying appendix includes a detailed derivation of a key estimate crucial to our findings. This work draws upon a comprehensive study of the blowup behavior of the given parabolic equation and its static counterpart, and a related problem involving a critical Hardy-Leray heat equation. The detailed examination of the blowup behavior of the given parabolic equation and its static counterpart, and a related problem involving a critical Hardy-Leray heat equation is the subject of a separate study. The detailed examination of the blowup behavior of the given parabolic equation and its static counterpart, and a related problem involving a critical Hardy-Leray heat equation is the subject of a separate study. The detailed examination of the blowup behavior of the given parabolic equation and its static counterpart, and a related problem involving a Hardy-Leray heat equation is the subject of a separate study. The detailed examination of the blowup behavior of the given parabolic equatio